    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mature oligodendrocytes from oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) have been shown to cause myelination of the central nervous system. In this study, we use optogenetic stimulation of the premotor cortex in awake, behaving mice to demonstrate that neuronal activity causes a robust proliferative response in OPCs and promotes subsequent oligodendrogenesis. Behaviorally relevant activity also increases myelination within the deep layers of the premotor cortex and subcortical fibers entering the corpus callosum. Interestingly, this neuronal activity–regulated oligodendrogenesis and myelination is correlated with improved motor function of the corresponding limb. Inhibition of oligodendrocyte differentiation blocks this improvement in behavior.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
